<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Edessa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/260">260 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-persian-wars">Roman–Persian Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Edessa was fought in 260 CE in upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> between the Roman emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a> and the Sasanian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a>. The Roman army was defeated and Valerian was captured, the first Roman emperor to be taken prisoner. Shapur commemorated the victory in rock reliefs, and it confirmed the power of the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-edessa.jpg" alt="Battle of Edessa" width="320" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naghsh-e_rostam,_Ir%C3%A1n,_2016-09-24,_DD_12.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Before the battle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a> had penetrated deep into Roman territory several times and had conquered and plundered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, in 253 or 256. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a>, having defeated the usurper Aemilianus and taken imperial power, reached the eastern provinces in 254 or 255 and gradually restored order. He then faced a naval Gothic invasion of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> ravaged Pontus and moved south into Cappadocia. An attempt by Valerian and his army at Antioch to intercept them failed because of plague. While the Roman army was in that weakened state, Shapur invaded northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> in 260, probably in early spring.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a>, then in his sixties, marched east to the Sasanian borders. According to Shapur's inscription at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaba-ye-zartosht">Ka'ba-ye Zartosht</a>, his army contained men from almost every part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> as well as Germanic allies. The armies met between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carrhae">Carrhae</a> and Edessa, and the Romans were thoroughly defeated.</p><p>Roman sources are unclear. They report that the army was beaten and besieged, that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a> tried to negotiate and was captured, and that his army may have surrendered after that. Some say Shapur broke his word and seized the emperor after agreeing to truce talks, but such accounts are typical of Roman descriptions of defeat, which blame the incompetence of generals and the treachery of foreigners. According to Ian Hughes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> lost about 10,000 in battle, and the rest of the army retreated to Edessa and surrendered to save their lives.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Accounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a>'s fate differ. Some scholars hold that Shapur sent him and part of his army to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishapur">Bishapur</a>, where they lived in relatively good conditions and worked as engineers, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/band-e-kaisar">Band-e Kaisar</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shushtar">Shushtar</a> is counted among the remains of their work. Lactantius says Shapur used the former emperor as a stepstool to mount his horse, Aurelius Victor says he was kept in a cage, and later stories say his body was skinned and stuffed as a trophy. Other accounts say he was treated with respect and that tales of torture were invented by Christian historians to show what befell persecutors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><p>Shapur then divided his army into at least five groups to raid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, ravaging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>, Cappadocia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamphylia">Pamphylia</a> and Lycaonia. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> and Caesarea Cappadocia, deporting the latter's people, claimed to number 400,000, to the southern provinces of his empire. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a>'s defeat set off revolts that led to the temporary fragmentation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-empire">Gallic Empire</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/postumus">Postumus</a> in the west.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Edessa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He fought three major wars against Rome, which culminated in the capture of Emperor Valerian at the <b>Battle of Edessa</b> in 260 CE, the only occasion on which a reigning Roman emperor was taken prisoner by a foreign power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
